1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,460
you know,I've been thinking about time travel again.

2
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:08,280
Why,did you hit a roadblock with invisibility?

3
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:11,840
Put it on the back burner.

4
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:15,390
Anyway,it occurs to me if I ever did perfect a time machine,

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,610
I would just go into the past and give it to myself,

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:21,270
thus eliminating the need for me to invent it in the first place.

7
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:22,980
Interesting.

8
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:25,170
Yeah,it really takes the pressure off.

9
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:27,790
Sounds like a breakthrough.

10
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:30,390
Should I call science magazine and tell them to hold the cover?

11
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,270
It's time travel,leonard,I will have already done that.

12
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,830
Then I guess congratulations are in order.

13
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:40,400
No,congratulations will have been in order.

14
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:42,940
You know,I am not going to enjoy this party.

15
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:45,490
I know,I'm familiar with you.

16
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,120
The last department party,professor finkleday cornered me

17
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,590
and talked about spelunking for 45 minutes.

18
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:51,880
Yes,I was there.

19
00:00:51,890 --> 00:00:53,800
You know what's interesting about caves,leonard?

20
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:55,500
- What? - Nothing.

21
00:00:57,100 --> 00:00:59,770
Well,then we'll avoid finkleday,we'll meet the new department head,

22
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:01,550
congratulate him,shake his hand and go.

23
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:02,690
How's this?

24
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,650
"Pleased to meet you,dr.Gablehauser.

25
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,620
"How fortunate for you that the university's "chosen to hire you,

26
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:10,970
despite the fact "that you've done no original research in 25 years,

27
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:13,140
"and instead have written a series of popular books "

28
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:16,080
that reduce the great concepts of science "to a series of anecdotes,

29
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:17,610
"each one dumbed down to accommodate the duration

30
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:21,260
of an average bowel movement.

31
00:01:21,990 --> 00:01:23,950
Mahalo.

32
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:29,290
"Mahalo's" a nice touch.

33
00:01:29,990 --> 00:01:32,330
You know there only eight consonants in the hawaiian language?

34
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:34,630
Interesting. You should lead with that.

35
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,740
Oh,god,look at this buffet.

36
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,410
I love america.

37
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,070
You don't have buffets in india?

38
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,450
Of course,but it's all indian food.

39
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:49,140
You can't find a bagel in mumbai to save your life.

40
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,060
Shmear me.

41
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:54,770
Well,here's an interesting turn of events.

42
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:56,430
What?

43
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,150
Howard brought a date?

44
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,860
A more plausible explanation is

45
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:04,100
that his work in robotics has made an amazing leap forward.

46
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,890
Hey,what up,science bitches?

47
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,220
May I introduce my special lady friend summer?

48
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,010
Howard,I told you touching's extra.

49
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:16,140
Right. Sorry.

50
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:19,380
Here comes our new boss. Be polite.

51
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:20,760
Hi,fellas.

52
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:22,800
- Eric gablehauser. - Howard wolowitz.

53
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:26,040
Howard,nice to meet you. And you are?

54
00:02:26,510 --> 00:02:29,880
An actual real scientist.

55
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:33,870
How was that?

56
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,490
I can't believe he fired me.

57
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:40,330
Well,you did call him a glorified high school science teacher

58
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:44,710
whose last successful experiment was lighting his own farts.

59
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:48,760
In my defense,I prefaced that by saying,"with all due respect."

60
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:12,090
The.Luminous.Fish.Effect

61
00:03:17,070 --> 00:03:18,340
Morning.

62
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:19,860
Morning.

63
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:22,000
You're making eggs for breakfast?

64
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:24,690
This isn't breakfast,it's an experiment.

65
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:28,900
'Cause it looks a lot like breakfast.

66
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:32,680
I finally have time to test my hypothesis about the separation of the water molecules

67
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:36,650
from the egg proteins and its impact vis-a-vis taste.

68
00:03:37,770 --> 00:03:39,900
Sounds yummy.

69
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,350
I look forward to your work with bacon.

70
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:44,290
As do I.

71
00:03:45,210 --> 00:03:47,880
You know,I'm sure if you just apologize to gablehauser,

72
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:49,600
he would give you your job back.

73
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:51,190
I don't want my job back.

74
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:55,180
I've spent the past three and a half years staring at grease boards full of equations.

75
00:03:55,190 --> 00:03:57,750
Before that,I spent four years working on my thesis.

76
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,870
Before that,I was in college,and before that,I was in the fifth grade.

77
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:06,740
This is my first d oayff in decades and I'm going to savor it.

78
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:08,740
Okay.

79
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:10,820
I'll let you get back to fixing your eggs.

80
00:04:10,830 --> 00:04:14,470
Not just fixing my eggs,I'm fixing everyone's eggs.

81
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:17,040
And we all thank you.

82
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:50,560
use new eggs.

83
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:55,220
hi. Hey,I'm running out to the market.

84
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:56,640
Do you guys need anything?

85
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:59,180
Oh,well,this would be one of those circumstances that

86
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:02,960
people unfamiliar with the law of large numbers would call a coincidence.

87
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:03,990
I'm sorry?

88
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:04,970
I need eggs.

89
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:06,440
Four dozen should suffice.

90
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:07,200
Four dozen?

91
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:09,530
Yes,and evenly distributed amongst brown,white,

92
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:12,820
free-range,large,extra large and jumbo.

93
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:14,200
Okay,one more time.

94
00:05:14,210 --> 00:05:16,530
Never mind. You won't get it right.

95
00:05:16,540 --> 00:05:18,690
I'd better come with you.

96
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:21,120
Oh,yay.

97
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:27,100
How come you didn't go into work today?

98
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:31,980
I'm taking a sabbatical because I won't kowtow to mediocre minds.

99
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:34,100
So you got canned,huh?

100
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:37,610
Theoretical physicists do not get canned...

101
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:39,490
but yeah.

102
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:41,480
Well,maybe it's all for the best.

103
00:05:41,490 --> 00:05:45,090
You know,I always say when one door closes,another one opens.

104
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:46,930
No,it doesn'T.

105
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:49,960
Not unless the two doors are connected by relays

106
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:51,700
or there are motion sensors involved.

107
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:52,300
No,no,I meant...

108
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:54,490
or if the first door closing creates a change of air pressure

109
00:05:54,490 --> 00:05:56,680
that acts upon the second door.

110
00:05:57,370 --> 00:05:59,290
Never mind.

111
00:06:00,090 --> 00:06:01,260
Slow down.

112
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:03,200
Slow down. Please,slow down!

113
00:06:03,210 --> 00:06:04,160
We're fine!

114
00:06:04,170 --> 00:06:06,810
You're not leaving yourself enough space between cars.

115
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:07,790
Oh,sure,I am.

116
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:09,630
No,no,let me do the math for you.

117
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,130
This car weighs,let's say 4,000 pounds.

118
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:14,530
Now add 140 for me,120 for you...

119
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:16,460
- 120? - Oh,I'm sorry.

120
00:06:16,470 --> 00:06:17,650
Did I insult you?

121
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:20,710
Is your body mass somehow tied into your self-worth?

122
00:06:21,090 --> 00:06:22,990
Well,yeah.

123
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,250
Interesting.

124
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:27,800
Anyway,that gives us a total weight of,let's say 4,400 pounds.

125
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:29,990
Let's say 4,390.

126
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,470
Fine.

127
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,430
We're traveling forward at-- good lord-- 51 miles an hour.

128
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:38,780
Now let's assume that your brakes are new and the calipers are aligned.

129
00:06:38,790 --> 00:06:40,520
Still,by the time we come to a stop,

130
00:06:40,530 --> 00:06:43,110
we'll be occupying the same space as that buick in front of us,

131
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:47,050
an impossibility that nature will quickly resolve into death,mutilation...

132
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:50,340
oh,look,they built a new putt-putt course.

133
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:54,960
This is great.

134
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:56,110
Look at me.

135
00:06:56,120 --> 00:06:59,480
I'm in the real world of ordinary people

136
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:02,120
just living their ordinary,colorless workaday lives.

137
00:07:02,590 --> 00:07:03,700
Thank you.

138
00:07:03,710 --> 00:07:05,650
No,thank you.

139
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:08,270
And thank you,ordinary person.

140
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:12,370
Hey,you want to hear an interesting thing about tomatoes?

141
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:13,750
Uh,no,no,not really.

142
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:16,830
Listen,didn't you say you needed some eggs?

143
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:20,210
Yes,but anyone who knows anything about the dynamics of bacterial growth

144
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,610
knows to pick up their refrigerated foods on the way out of the supermarket.

145
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:27,120
Oh,okay,well,then maybe you should start heading on out then.

146
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,630
No,this is fun.

147
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:32,230
Oh,the thing about tomatoes-- and I think you'll really enjoy this--

148
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:36,130
is they're shelved with the vegetables,but they're technically a fruit.

149
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:37,680
- Oh,interesting. - Isn't it?

150
00:07:37,690 --> 00:07:40,520
No,I mean what you find enjoyable.

151
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:46,210
Oh,boy.

152
00:07:47,350 --> 00:07:49,310
What now?

153
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:51,750
Well,there's some value to taking a multivitamin,

154
00:07:51,750 --> 00:07:54,180
but the human body can only absorb so much.

155
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:58,220
What you're buying here are the ingredients for very expensive urine.

156
00:07:59,910 --> 00:08:03,050
Well,maybe that's what I was going for.

157
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:06,170
Well,then you'll want some manganese.

158
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:13,170
Well,that was fun.

159
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:15,900
Maybe tomorrow we can go to one of those big warehouse stores.

160
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:17,060
Oh,I don't know,sheldon.

161
00:08:17,070 --> 00:08:20,890
It's going to take me a while to recover from all the fun I had today.

162
00:08:21,890 --> 00:08:22,810
Are you sure?

163
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:25,300
There are a lot of advantages to buying in bulk.

164
00:08:25,310 --> 00:08:30,430
For example,I noticed that you purchase your tampons one-month supply at a time.

165
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:33,540
What?

166
00:08:33,550 --> 00:08:34,200
Think about it.

167
00:08:34,210 --> 00:08:35,960
It's a product that doesn't spoil

168
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:38,890
and you're going to be needing them for at least the next 30 years.

169
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:43,260
You want me to buy 30 years worth of tampons?

170
00:08:43,270 --> 00:08:46,340
Well,30,35. When did your mother go into menopause?

171
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:47,160
Okay.

172
00:08:47,930 --> 00:08:49,930
I'm not talking about this with you.

173
00:08:49,940 --> 00:08:52,600
Oh,penny,this is a natural human process

174
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:55,020
and we're talking about statistically significant savings.

175
00:08:55,030 --> 00:08:59,070
Now,if you assume 15 tampons per cycle and a 28-day cycle...

176
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:01,650
are you fairly regular?

177
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:09,720
Okay,no warehouse store,but we're still on for putt-putt golf,right?

178
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:16,550
Hey,I just ran into penny.

179
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:18,620
She seemed upset about something.

180
00:09:18,630 --> 00:09:21,050
I think it's her time of the month.

181
00:09:22,930 --> 00:09:26,100
I marked the calendar for future reference.

182
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:31,210
What's with the fish?

183
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:33,220
It's an experiment.

184
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:36,310
What happened to your scrambled egg research?

185
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:37,660
Oh,that was a dead end.

186
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:40,810
Scrambled eggs are as good as they're ever going to be.

187
00:09:41,210 --> 00:09:43,980
So... fish?

188
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:47,030
I read an article about japanese scientists

189
00:09:47,030 --> 00:09:49,770
who inserted dna from luminous jellyfish into other animals

190
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:54,210
and I thought,"hey,fish nightlights.

191
00:09:56,410 --> 00:09:58,300
Fish nightlights?

192
00:09:58,310 --> 00:09:59,920
It's a billion-dollar idea.

193
00:10:02,370 --> 00:10:04,200
Mum's the word.

194
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,980
Sheldon,are you sure you don't want to

195
00:10:06,980 --> 00:10:09,350
just apologize to gablehauser and get your job back?

196
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:12,490
Oh,no,no,no,I have too much to do.

197
00:10:13,110 --> 00:10:14,500
Like luminous fish.

198
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:16,860
I'm sorry. I didn'T...

199
00:10:18,650 --> 00:10:20,220
that's just the beginning.

200
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:24,510
I also have an idea for a bulk mail-order feminine hygiene company.

201
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:27,010
Oh,glow-in-the-dark tampons!

202
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,670
Leonard,we're gonna be rich.

203
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:37,990
Thanks for coming on such short notice.

204
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,300
Well,you did the right thing calling.

205
00:10:39,310 --> 00:10:40,440
I didn't know what else to do.

206
00:10:40,450 --> 00:10:41,750
He's lost all focus.

207
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:44,200
Every day he's got a new obsession.

208
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:53,990
This is a particularly disturbing one.

209
00:10:56,150 --> 00:10:57,970
Mommy?

210
00:10:58,190 --> 00:11:00,840
Hi,baby!

211
00:11:10,810 --> 00:11:14,510
Oh,you got yourself a loom? How nice!

212
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:15,650
Thank you.

213
00:11:15,660 --> 00:11:18,070
Honey,why'd you get a loom?

214
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:23,050
I was working with luminous fish,and I thought,hey...

215
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:26,280
"loom."

216
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:30,080
Mom,what are you doing here?

217
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:31,750
Leonard called me.

218
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:33,630
I know,but why?

219
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:36,890
Because one of the great minds of the 21st century

220
00:11:36,890 --> 00:11:40,390
is raising glow-in-the-dark fish and weaving serapes.

221
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:43,040
This is not a serape,this is a poncho.

222
00:11:43,050 --> 00:11:45,250
A serape is open at the sides; a poncho is closed.

223
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:46,180
This is a poncho.

224
00:11:46,190 --> 00:11:50,060
And neither is a reason to call someone's mother.

225
00:11:50,910 --> 00:11:53,990
Really? When was the last time you left the house?

226
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,690
I went to the market with penny.

227
00:11:55,700 --> 00:11:57,570
Yeah,that was three weeks ago.

228
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:02,110
Well,then buckle up-- in the next four to eight days she's going to get very crabby.

229
00:12:04,550 --> 00:12:07,420
Sweetheart,your little friend is concerned about you.

230
00:12:07,430 --> 00:12:12,130
Yes,well,I'm not a child-- I'm a grown man,capable of living my life as I see fit.

231
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:16,060
And I certainly don't need someone telling on me to my mother!

232
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:18,020
Wait,where are you going?

233
00:12:18,030 --> 00:12:20,910
To my room-- and no one's allowed in!

234
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:25,540
oh,he gets his temper from his daddy.

235
00:12:25,550 --> 00:12:28,260
- He's got my eyes. - I see.

236
00:12:28,270 --> 00:12:31,770
All that science stuff,that comes from jesus.

237
00:12:34,930 --> 00:12:36,620
Sheldon,your mom made dinner.

238
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:38,390
I'm not hungry!

239
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:40,730
Oh,leonard,don't trouble yourself. He's stubborn.

240
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:43,180
He may stay in there till the rapture.

241
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:46,920
We so sure that's a bad thing?

242
00:12:46,930 --> 00:12:49,770
I tell you,I love the boy to death,but he has been difficult

243
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:52,340
since he fell out of me at the k-mart.

244
00:12:54,670 --> 00:13:00,310
Excuse me for being so bold,but I now see where sheldon gets his smoldering good looks.

245
00:13:01,730 --> 00:13:04,850
Honey,that ain't gonna work,but you keep tryin'.

246
00:13:05,410 --> 00:13:07,250
I made chicken.

247
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:12,360
I hope that isn't one of the animals that you people think is magic.

248
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:20,100
You know,we have an indian gentleman at our church,

249
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,650
a dr.Patel-- it's a beautiful story.

250
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:27,240
The lord spoke to him and moved him to give us all 20% off on lasik--

251
00:13:27,250 --> 00:13:29,690
you know,those that needed it.

252
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:31,190
That is a lovely story.

253
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:33,500
Um,are we gonna do anything about sheldon?

254
00:13:33,510 --> 00:13:34,360
Oh,we will.

255
00:13:34,370 --> 00:13:36,460
You have to take your time with sheldon.

256
00:13:36,470 --> 00:13:38,600
His father,god rest his soul,used to always say to me,

257
00:13:38,610 --> 00:13:41,830
"mary,you have to take your time with sheldon."

258
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:44,150
Sounds like a wise man.

259
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:45,720
Oh,not so wise.

260
00:13:45,730 --> 00:13:49,030
He once tried to fight a bobcat for some licorice.

261
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:55,000
So,everybody grab a plate and a pretty place mat that shelly wove.

262
00:13:55,290 --> 00:13:57,550
Has shelly ever freaked out like this before?

263
00:13:57,560 --> 00:13:59,140
Oh,all the time.

264
00:13:59,150 --> 00:14:01,500
I remember one summer when he was 13,

265
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:04,840
he built a small nuclear reactor in the shed

266
00:14:04,850 --> 00:14:08,060
and told everybody he was gonna provide free electricity for the whole town.

267
00:14:08,070 --> 00:14:13,000
Well,the only problem was,he had no what you call "fissionable" materials.

268
00:14:13,410 --> 00:14:16,420
Anyway,when he went on the internets to get some,

269
00:14:16,430 --> 00:14:21,090
a man from the government came by and sat him down real gentle,

270
00:14:21,130 --> 00:14:25,590
and told him it's against the law to have yellow-cake uranium in a shed.

271
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:26,970
Well,what happened?

272
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:28,510
Well,poor boy had a fit.

273
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:32,700
Locked himself in his room and built a sonic death ray.

274
00:14:32,710 --> 00:14:33,670
A death ray?

275
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:35,150
Well,that's what he called it.

276
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:37,640
Didn't even slow down the neighbor kids.

277
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:41,020
It pissed our dog off to no end.

278
00:14:42,890 --> 00:14:45,760
You know,you two make a cute couple.

279
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:47,860
uh,no. We're not...

280
00:14:47,870 --> 00:14:49,350
we're not-not a couple. We're singles.

281
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:50,170
- Two singles. - Yeah.

282
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:54,240
Like those... individually wrapped slices of cheese that...

283
00:14:55,830 --> 00:14:57,510
we're friends.

284
00:14:58,700 --> 00:14:59,820
Did I pluck a nerve there?

285
00:14:59,830 --> 00:15:01,360
- Oh,yeah. - Okay.

286
00:15:01,370 --> 00:15:03,750
All right,everybody,it's time to eat.

287
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:09,520
Oh,lord,we thank you for this meal and all of your bounty.

288
00:15:09,530 --> 00:15:13,290
And we pray that you help sheldon get back on his rocker.

289
00:15:14,110 --> 00:15:18,730
Now,after a moment of silent meditation,I'm gonna end with "in jesus' name,"

290
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:22,020
but you two don't feel any obligation to join in.

291
00:15:22,910 --> 00:15:25,930
Unless,of course,the holy spirit moves you.

292
00:15:35,130 --> 00:15:37,990
Oh,my god,this is the best cobbler I've ever had.

293
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,740
It was always sheldon's favorite.

294
00:15:39,750 --> 00:15:42,120
You know w thathe secret ingredient is?

295
00:15:42,130 --> 00:15:44,350
- Love? - Lard.

296
00:15:48,930 --> 00:15:49,980
Hey,look who's come...

297
00:15:50,610 --> 00:15:52,360
You'll spook him.

298
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:57,100
He's like a baby deer-- you got to let him come to you.

299
00:16:05,490 --> 00:16:06,720
This is ridiculous.

300
00:16:06,730 --> 00:16:08,910
Damn it,sheldon,snap out of it!

301
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:10,380
You're a physicist--

302
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:13,530
you belong at the university doing research,not hiding in your room!

303
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:22,660
you don't hunt,do you?

304
00:16:28,350 --> 00:16:30,550
good morning,snickerdoodle.

305
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:32,560
Morning.

306
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:35,880
Oh,well,that looks awful fancy. What is that?

307
00:16:37,070 --> 00:16:41,570
It's my idea of what dna would look like in a silicon-based life-form.

308
00:16:42,810 --> 00:16:46,740
But intelligently designed by a creator,right?

309
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:50,670
What do you want,mom?

310
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:53,000
You know how your daddy used to say that

311
00:16:53,010 --> 00:16:56,090
you can only fish for so long

312
00:16:56,090 --> 00:16:57,630
before you got to throw a stick of dynamite in the water?

313
00:16:58,020 --> 00:16:58,730
Yeah.

314
00:16:58,740 --> 00:17:01,170
Well,I'm done fishing.

315
00:17:02,690 --> 00:17:03,980
You put those on.

316
00:17:03,990 --> 00:17:04,770
What for?

317
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:06,860
Because you're gonna go down to your office,

318
00:17:06,870 --> 00:17:09,710
you're gonna apologize to your boss and get your job back.

319
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:10,570
No.

320
00:17:10,580 --> 00:17:15,110
I'm sorry,did I start that sentence with the words,"if it please your highness"?

321
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:19,870
I'm not going to apologize-- I didn't say anything that wasn't true.

322
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:21,370
Now,you listen here.

323
00:17:21,380 --> 00:17:24,120
I have been telling you since you were four years old,

324
00:17:24,130 --> 00:17:28,110
it's okay to be smarter than everybody,but you can't go around pointing it out.

325
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:28,860
Why not?

326
00:17:28,870 --> 00:17:31,400
Because people don't like it!

327
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:36,040
Remember all the ass-kickings you got from the neighbor kids?

328
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,950
now,let's get crackin'.

329
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:43,200
Shower,shirt,shoes,and let's shove off.

330
00:17:45,450 --> 00:17:49,390
There wouldn't have been any ass-kickings if that stupid death ray had worked.

331
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,000
Problem solved.

332
00:17:56,310 --> 00:17:57,400
Really?

333
00:17:57,410 --> 00:17:58,720
That's impressive.

334
00:17:58,730 --> 00:18:02,150
Leonard,the lord never gives us more than we can handle.

335
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:06,080
Thankfully,he blessed me with two other children who are dumb as soup.

336
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:13,970
Excuse me,dr.Gablehauser,are you busy?

337
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:14,590
Well,actually...

338
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:16,020
sheldon,he's just doodlin'.

339
00:18:16,030 --> 00:18:17,490
Get in here.

340
00:18:20,890 --> 00:18:21,850
Dr.Gablehauser.

341
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:23,820
Dr.Cooper.

342
00:18:24,560 --> 00:18:27,110
Let's go,baby-- we're losing daylight.

343
00:18:31,350 --> 00:18:35,650
as you know,several weeks ago in our first encounter,

344
00:18:35,660 --> 00:18:40,110
we may have gotten off on the wrong foot when I called you an idiot.

345
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:43,460
And I just wanted to say that I was wrong...

346
00:18:43,470 --> 00:18:46,010
to point it out.

347
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:50,520
I'm sorry,we haven't been introduced.

348
00:18:50,530 --> 00:18:51,720
Dr.Eric gablehauser.

349
00:18:51,730 --> 00:18:53,700
Mary cooper,sheldon's mom.

350
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:55,190
Now,that's impossible--

351
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:57,220
you must have had him when you were a teenager.

352
00:18:57,230 --> 00:18:58,470
Oh,aren't you sweet.

353
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:00,730
His father's dead.

354
00:19:02,350 --> 00:19:04,520
- Recently? - Long enough.

355
00:19:05,250 --> 00:19:06,560
Please.

356
00:19:07,650 --> 00:19:09,980
sheldon,shouldn't you be working?

357
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:15,940
Okay.

358
00:19:20,390 --> 00:19:22,130
Hey,how did it go?

359
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:23,670
I got my job back.

360
00:19:23,680 --> 00:19:25,750
Really? What happened?

361
00:19:26,130 --> 00:19:28,040
I'm not quite sure.

362
00:19:28,730 --> 00:19:32,700
It involves a part of the human experience that has always eluded me.

363
00:19:33,490 --> 00:19:35,930
Well,that narrows it down.

364
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:41,920
I'm very proud of you,honey.

365
00:19:41,930 --> 00:19:44,680
You showed a lot of courage today.

366
00:19:44,690 --> 00:19:46,280
Thanks,mom.

367
00:19:49,990 --> 00:19:51,130
Mom?

368
00:19:52,110 --> 00:19:55,740
Is dr.Gablehauser going to be my new daddy?

369
00:19:58,380 --> 00:20:00,080
We'll see.

370
00:20:00,090 --> 00:20:01,180
Sleep tight.

